      "id": "sampling-clock-and-estimation-route",
      "prompt": "Use the engineering writing Skill to revise an Introduction closing paragraph, one contribution, and a Methodology opening for a fictional industrial soft sensor. Process measurements arrive every 5 s or every 20 s, while laboratory concentration targets are much less frequent. A previously verified comparator retains only times when every process variable is observed. The proposed probabilistic model learns a common state from whichever variables were actually observed, using a measurement equation restricted to that observed subset. It does not fill a complete measurement grid before fitting or predicting. Training uses concentration targets only at their observed times. The trained predictor estimates concentration at each 5 s decision time from observations already received; the current concentration assay is not available. The implemented change is the observed-subset likelihood with a supervised state-to-quality relation, not a new state-space encoder. A held-out chronological comparison reports lower concentration RMSE than the complete-observation comparator with the same measurements and training labels. Current prose says: 'The second stream has a fourfold higher sampling rate. All missing samples are recovered to eliminate information loss, after which the model learns quality features. Our framework is novel because it contains an encoder, a regression layer and an experimental study.' Keep the actual scientific meaning, give coherent positive English prose instead of a checklist, and add brief author notes explaining material changes. Do not invent numerical performance values or citations. No edits to any real manuscript are requested.",
      "skill": "engineering-application-journal-writing-skill",
