      "id": "training-smoothing-and-online-filtering",
      "prompt": "Please draft an approximately 160-190-word English abstract and a short contribution paragraph for a fictional quality-estimation study. Measurements contain variation related to concentration and other variation. The model represents both through two interacting latent components in the same measurement equation; only the first has a direct concentration-output equation. During TRAIN fitting, forward-backward smoothing uses the complete TRAIN sequence, including TRAIN concentration assays, to estimate latent-state moments for parameter learning. TEST runs are disjoint from TRAIN. After training, model parameters are fixed, a forward filter conditions on process observations available up to each prediction time, and the concentration head uses the estimated quality-linked state. TEST concentration values are never filter inputs; they are later used only for scoring. The auxiliary state helps explain the process observations used for joint inference, rather than being discarded. The novel contribution is adapting the joint observation model to varying observed-variable subsets, so both fast and slow streams contribute without deleting incomplete observations. A matched held-out production study improves concentration RMSE over the complete-observation model; no change in control performance or deployed operation was measured. Please make the link from acquisition difficulty to model operation and evaluated output clear. Do not describe all components as separately novel. End with at most two author notes if needed; do not add a future-work list or make up results.",
